Here's the complete migration analysis and replacement code.

## Pattern Classification

| Location | Pattern | Issue on .NET 8 |
|---|---|---|
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | `SYSLIB0046` (obsolete in .NET 9, unreliable in .NET 8) |
| `_thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop fallback | `PlatformNotSupportedException` — **will crash** |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call that needs cancellation | Can't be interrupted by token without `WaitAny` |
| `while (true)` | Cancellable work loop | No cooperative exit mechanism |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent, token.WaitHandle };

        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny returns immediately when EITHER the event is
            // signaled OR the token is cancelled — replaces both
            // Thread.Interrupt and Thread.Abort wake-up behavior.
            int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (index == 1) // CancellationToken fired
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item != null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait for clean exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Key Changes

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `while (true)` | `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)` | Cooperative exit |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles)` | Cancellable blocking |
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Token wakes `WaitAny` |
| `_thread.Abort()` | *(removed)* | Impossible on .NET 8 |
| No cleanup | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` must be disposed |

## Pitfalls to Watch

1. **Race on `Dequeue`**: The original code crashes if `Set()` fires but the queue is empty (spurious wake). The migrated version guards with `_queue.Count > 0`.
2. **`CancellationTokenSource` leak**: It's `IDisposable` — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. Added `IDisposable` to the class.
3. **`Abort` after `Join` timeout**: The original falls back to `Abort()` which **throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** on .NET 8. With cooperative cancellation, `Join(5000)` is sufficient — the loop exits promptly once the token fires.
4. **`Thread.Interrupt` on .NET 9+**: Even if it works today on .NET 8, it's `SYSLIB0046`-obsolete starting in .NET 9. Don't use it as a bridge.

> **Optional further modernization**: Consider replacing the entire `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` pattern with `System.Threading.Channels.Channel<string>`, which handles all the synchronization internally. That's a design improvement, not a Thread.Abort migration requirement.
